                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "m7":</h3><p><a href="/images/mic_extras/gefell/gefell-M7-capsule.jpg" rel="shadowbox"><img src="/images/mic_extras/gefell/gefell-M7-capsule-sm.jpg" width="100" alt="Gefell M7 capsule" class="thumb shaft" /></a><b>M7</b> designates a type of condenser microphone capsule manufactured by <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Microtech-Gefell">Microtech Gefell</a> in the former East Germany. This was the original capsule found on Neumann&#8217;s U-47 microphone.</p><p>The primary distinguishing characteristic of the M7 is that the diaphragm is made of PVC plastic rather than polyester (Mylar) film. The plastic is poured and cooled, forming a material ranging from 8-12 microns in thickness. This is thicker than typical Mylar diaphragms (of 3-9 microns).</p><p>As of 2003, Gefell published that its PVC diaphragms were 10 microns thick. In 2009, noted microphone technician Andreas Grosser claimed that the original M7 diaphragms varied from 10&ndash;12&micro;m thick.</p><p>Each M7 capsule consists of two PVC membranes that are glued to a single, shared backplate. This assembly process is also somewhat unique to the M7, in that most other condenser microphone capsules use screwed-down tension rings to attach and tune their diaphragms.</p><p>The M7 backplate measures 32mm in diameter; the diameter of the suspended portion of the membrane is 25mm. The two diaphragms are center-terminated.</p><p>Neumann (Berlin) stopped producing the M7 in 1958. As of this writing, the only two sources for PVC capsules are Microtech Gefell and ex-MTG technician <a href="http://www.thiersch-mic.de/">Sigfried Thiersch</a> (whose STW7 is available with either diaphragms made of either Mylar or 6-micron PVC).</p><p>Read Klaus Heyne&#8217;s <a href="http://recforums.prosoundweb.com/index.php/m/75518/0">History of the M7 Capsule</a>, and his <a href="http://recforums.prosoundweb.com/index.php/t/26490/18521/">2009 - State of the M7</a> report.</p><p>Note that <a href="http://recforums.prosoundweb.com/index.php/m/0/10703/32/0/#msg_num_12">early Neumann microphones were equipped with M7 capsules</a> as well, from 1949 until about 1960, when Neumann began using the K47 capsule in place of the M7. (The K47 retained the M7&#8217;s single-backplate architecture, but swapped Mylar for PVC as a membrane material, and attached the membranes via a screwed-down tensioning ring rather than with glue.)</p><p>The black ring around the circumference of the capsule in the photo above is a &#8220;protection cover [used] to [create] better isolation between electrode and membrane.&#8221;</p><p>Read <a href="http://www.andreas-grosser.com/2009/10/m7-capsule-article-by-ag/">Andreas Grosser&#8217;s M7 recollections</a>.</p>
